ere they were  the last time we were there  the sunrise looks amazing and does a  great job to activate that sense of  wonder about leaving the planet earth  that pretty much everyone has had from  the time you start looking up at the  night sky  it's especially wondrous for the first  timers and the conversation where molly  is telling the dutch astronaut who's  based off the first real dutchman in  space  to stop looking at his screen and enjoy  the view  highlights one of the things that i  really love about this it shows how  going to the moon  will eventually just become another  thing we do it'll become a job for these  people  the veterans molly and ellen are already  feeling that  part of why space exploration is so  fascinating  is because we haven't done that much of  it yet we're arriving at the period in  the show's timeline where going to the  moon is just another thing we do  over 10 years because of the ongoing  space race with the soviet union things  have progressed at a rapid pace  we get a better idea of what it all  looks like when we go back to earth but  before we get into that i just want to  say that the idea of closing this  segment off on a high note with the  wonder of the sunrise  really pays off later when we return we  go back  24 hours in time and see margot wake up  in her office which is a nice call back  to her introduction in the series  and again there are plenty of details to  mark the differences from when we last  saw her  she's got photos of shuttle launches  hanging on her wall  a 20-year service award and she's got  this huge  office telling us she continued to  advance in her position  at a nasa that's moving its space  program forward at an accelerated pace  that's all good because margot is one of  my favorite characters  and after we visit her we see what's  going on with the rest of them ed  baldwin is now the chief of astronauts  he stepped into deke slayton's old role  on top of the really sweet photo he has  a molly 